3 #uuid "73bd025a-b93e-50f2-9bdf-0861517b1727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228561 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3214b49d-a843-5dfc-aaf4-826626ec3984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228562 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t) = 22.5 - 16.5e^{-t/25}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228562 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228562 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "99808cf8-bbc9-54ff-a1ba-83f2a5fdeb6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228563 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t) = 22.5 - 16.5e^{-t/20}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228563 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228563 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5521b71-2ca3-5f43-b4af-fa177f129bcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228564 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t) = 22.5 - 16.5e^{t/25}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228564 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228564 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ed98ee46-bedb-5d89-9d89-4c2aa24d8721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228565 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t) = 6 - 22.5e^{-t/25}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228565 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228565 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ce4aff0-803e-599f-a905-bde82047bc35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228566 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(t) = 22.5 + 16.5e^{-t/25}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228566 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228566 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e116b4e-4acb-509c-b36b-4565f709f143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228930 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228930 #edb/ref 1071 "A water-treatment holding tank initially holds $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved coagulant.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f5d8d59-2cc7-53bf-92ad-6062df331851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228930 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (70+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =1.5(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =1.5(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ 0.5(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =12 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-23 \\\\ C & =-23(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)-\\frac{23(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.5(70+t)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.5(70+8)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+8})}^{2} \\\\  & =39-23{(\\frac{70}{78})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈20.48\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228931 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228931 #edb/ref 1071 "A syrup mixing tank in a confectionery factory can hold up to $240\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of syrup solution with $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated syrup with concentration $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbf602f0-1101-55d5-a6b3-9bfc5731e9ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228931 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (12+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (12+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(12+t)Q & =36(12+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2}) & =36(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(12+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ 12(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 12\\cdot 12+\\frac{C}{(12)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =-112 \\\\ C & =-112(12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(12+t)-\\frac{112(12)^{2}}{(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(12+t)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 240$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 96+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 96+8{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =144 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =18\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(18) & =12(12+18)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+18})}^{2} \\\\  & =360-112{(\\frac{12}{30})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈342.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228949 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228949 #edb/ref 1071 "A fertilizer tank initially holds $800\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of nutrient $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b62d830-be21-5734-8a12-115b6a5136bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228949 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\textrm{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{800}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q = 0.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $Q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nQ_c(t) = Ae^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nQ_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $Q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + \\dfrac{1}{20}\\alpha = 0.4\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = 8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $Q_p(t) = 8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t) = Q_c(t) + Q_p(t) = Ae^{-t/20} + 8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 7,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+8 & =7 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 8 - e^{-t/20}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229181 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229181 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229181 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229181 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229724 #edb/