/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229183 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229183 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229183 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229183 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229183 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c652164a-2e94-5e16-a410-5143037af134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling Mixture Problems With First-Order Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186230082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229261 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "51694459-f27a-580f-878c-371665540655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1071 "A de-icing salt reservoir can hold up to $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine with $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A stronger brine with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98f92dde-10ee-5a2e-ae0c-afe47e1981a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229693 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of brine in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; brine enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed brine leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=2(90+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4.8-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q=4.8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(90+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(90+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(90+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(90+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q) & =(90+t)^{2}\\cdot 4.8 \\\\ (90+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(90+t)Q & =4.8(90+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2}) & =4.8(90+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 4.8(90+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(90+t)^{2} & =4.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(90+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)+\\frac{C}{(90+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=54,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =54 \\\\ 1.6(90+0)+\\frac{C}{(90+0)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{(90)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ C & =-90(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)-\\frac{90(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.6(90+t)-90{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 2(90+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 90+{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =1.6(90+60)-90{(\\frac{90}{90+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =240-90{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =207.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229698 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229698 #edb/ref 1071 "A storage tank initially holds $600L$ of liquid containing $5kg$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10L/min$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "310f29d9-e5f9-5829-9c84-72d1324e1777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229698 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229724 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229724 #edb/ref 1071 "A wastewater basin initially holds $750L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved pollutant.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved pollutant is pumped into the basin at a rate of $30L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. Which expression gives the amount of pollutant $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the basin $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229724 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "872cac41-3a52-503a-bac8-64cc582ee5f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230082 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230082 #edb/ref 1061 601750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230082 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70852def-fb14-50cc-b88d-6fe2af2343bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230082 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230083 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230083 #edb/ref 1061 601751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "844e4313-21e2-55d1-b55c-10160b652b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230084 #edb/ref 1061 601754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "926db8f3-a67c-5f3b-9bd8-4eaeaa70c7cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230085 #edb/ref 1061 601757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230085 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ff264ca2-0325-5ecb-9be4-8cbee9d9ba83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230085 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230205 #edb/ref 1064 "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ describes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate: $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $\\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\ & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\ & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem: $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230205 #edb/ref 1065 14578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230205 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fc8b0fc6-c25c-50f5-adbd-028adf7c6698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230205 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377192 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "33da174c-337a-5186-96b2-62c3b16e65a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377193 #edb/ref 1080 "201.0\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377193 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377193 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ac342bc-5d31-58a3-842a-8a4e79ae42dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377194 #edb/ref 1080 "204.3\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2980a552-e3d5-5564-ab0e-1bbb2358ce8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377195 #edb/ref 1080 "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377195 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377195 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377196 #edb/ref 1080 "206.2\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377196 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377196 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49a8983c-5113-540c-9960-edc50602eed7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377197 #edb/ref 1080 "205.4\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377197 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377197 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c1780f1-fe83-536d-9909-81d9b471da59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377205 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377205 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377205 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377205 #edb/ref 1071 "A flavoring tank at a beverage factory can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of flavoring solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved flavoring. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377205 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47a16983-dbf1-596b-b57d-9f38439fdd0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377205 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which flavoring enters the tank minus the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of flavoring enters the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *flavoring* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=75+9t-6t=3(25+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *flavoring* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(25+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|25+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(25+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q) & =(25+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (25+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(25+t)Q & =6(25+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2}) & =6(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(25+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(25+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(25+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(25+t)+\\frac{C}{(25+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of flavoring. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(25+0)+\\frac{C}{(25+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 50+\\frac{C}{(25)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{25}^{2}} & =-25 \\\\ C & =-25(25)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(25+t)-\\frac{25(25)^{2}}{(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(25+t)-25{(\\frac{25}{25+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 75+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 75+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =75 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2(25+25)-25{(\\frac{25}{25+25})}^{2} \\\\  & =100-25{(\\frac{25}{50})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈93.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377206 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "56a930be-e052-5424-bf3a-7194b15d9c7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377207 #edb/ref 1080 "93.3\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377207 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377207 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d60df00e-8828-5ca8-b2ed-d8bd599eb07f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377208 #edb/ref 1080 "92.9\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377208 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377208 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eaa07fe2-d9eb-58e7-adb1-02efd7fc679e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377209 #edb/ref 1080 "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377209 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377209 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377210 #edb/ref 1080 "94.5\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377210 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377210 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1dbf4199-9fdf-5b11-9f3c-4a9a9611b20f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377211 #edb/ref 1080 "94.1\\,\\text{lb}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377211 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377211 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6800f5a-8ca4-5d4d-a314-681c9b7bdc6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377263 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377263 #edb/ref 1071 "A storage tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved disinfectant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of disinfectant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e940f8bd-b1c9-5052-8127-7c721b39ac69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377263 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of disinfectant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =3 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377264 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377266 #edb/ref 1