ener $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377300 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35a9dbe2-5b3d-52d0-b235-b8068a9e0f15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377300 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sweetener enters the tank minus the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sweetener enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sweetener* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $24\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+24t-32t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sweetener in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sweetener* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(32\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(32\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q=1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-4}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot 1.2 \\\\ (30-t)^{-4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(30-t)^{-5}Q & =1.2(30-t)^{-4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4}) & =1.2(30-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4})\\,dt & =\\int 1.2(30-t)^{-4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-4} & =1.2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30-t)^{-3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)+C(30-t)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of sweetener. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ C(30-0)^{4}+0.4(30-0) & =7 \\\\ C(30)^{4}+12 & =7 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}(30-t)^{4} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 30\\,(\\frac{30-t}{30})-5{(\\frac{30-t}{30})}^{4} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})-5{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-5,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-5)=17.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377301 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5c722bf6-15d0-5bfc-a832-c8c844cbaa11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377302 #edb/ref 1080 "20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377302 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377302 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f681e08e-700d-5f11-a546-1b73d4a5d646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377303 #edb/ref 1080 "26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377303 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377303 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b28e0e34-3fc5-53eb-9f95-3e5de6d384b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377304 #edb/ref 1080 "14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377304 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377304 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b12db81-cdb5-5c78-aff0-867122be9ffa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377305 #edb/ref 1080 "17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377306 #edb/ref 1080 "29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87a222e1-6c03-558b-9d4f-e7aa60b98bea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377314 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377314 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377314 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377314 #edb/ref 1071 "A fermentation vat initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient is pumped into the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377314 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46db6f1f-9edd-53d3-9387-f26ef7512b99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377314 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $10\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+10t-5t=(500+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the vat, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & =100+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=100+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (100+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.6(100+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)) & =0.6(100+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(100+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t) & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(100+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)+\\frac{C}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=8,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =8 \\\\ 0.3(100+0)+\\frac{C}{100+0} & =8 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{100} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{C}{100} & =-22 \\\\ C & =-2200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)-\\frac{2200}{100+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.3(100+8)-\\frac{2200}{100+8} \\\\  & =32.4-\\frac{2200}{108} \\\\  & ≈12.03\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377315 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "70d825b7-f752-5a4c-9bbe-c55b7b40186e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377316 #edb/ref 1080 "11.58\\,\\text{kg}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377316 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377316 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "099acc92-7fac-5696-b31e-f1fb2df34af2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377317 #edb/ref 1080 "10.94\\,\\text{kg}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377317 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377317 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b84c947f-ccfb-56fc-b617-b376d1a795a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377318 #edb/ref 1080 "12.03\\,\\text{kg}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377318 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377318 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "595a8122-0ca3-5fa3-841a-170aadcc3604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377319 #edb/ref 1080 "12.67\\,\\text{kg}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377319 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377319 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d84483d6-8474-5c8e-8b9c-2b641cc22149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377320 #edb/ref 1080 "13.12\\,\\text{kg}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377320 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377320 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26b77d91-31cc-548b-acc6-bdc54133b6cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385215 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385215 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385215 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385215 #edb/ref 1071 "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $160\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d3bb996-2c5a-57e9-940a-6b48f2e05670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385215 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+6t-4t=2(50+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(50+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|50+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(50+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(50+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q) & =(50+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (50+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(50+t)Q & =6(50+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2}) & =6(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(50+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(50+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(50+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(50+t)+\\frac{C}{(50+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(50+0)+\\frac{C}{(50+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 100+\\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =-75 \\\\ C & =-75(50)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(50+t)-\\frac{75(50)^{2}}{(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(50+t)-75{(\\frac{50}{50+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $160\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=160$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =160 \\\\ 100+6{t}_{o}-4{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 100+2{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =2(50+30)-75{(\\frac{50}{50+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =160-75{(\\frac{50}{80})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈130.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385216 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9f7d8e34-4951-5d39-86d9-577